::CW_U05_L06_Q01::Which author presentation opening is strongest?{
~A. My piece has a lot of things in it.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not frame the audience.
=B. "Halfway Home" is a short poem meant to show a speaker deciding whether to return or move forward.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose.
~C. I will explain every line before you read it.#Incorrect. This overexplains and may replace the creative work.
~D. The piece is finished, so no explanation matters.#Incorrect. A presentation should prepare the audience.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q02::What is craft reasoning?{
~A. Listing every event in the plot.#Incorrect. Plot summary is not craft reasoning.
~B. Saying the piece is good.#Incorrect. This does not explain a specific choice.
~C. Reading the title only.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning requires explanation.
=D. Explaining why a writer made a specific choice and how it affects the reader.#Correct. This defines craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q03::Which sentence best explains a craft choice?{
=A. I used short sentences during the argument so the scene would feel tense and quick.#Correct. This names the choice and explains reader effect.
~B. I wrote sentences because writing needs sentences.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. My piece has punctuation.#Incorrect. This does not explain why the punctuation matters.
~D. I like my story.#Incorrect. This is personal opinion, not craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q04::Which presentation should be revised because it is mostly summary?{
~A. I wanted the poem to show hesitation, so I used pauses before the final line.#Incorrect. This explains purpose and craft.
~B. I revised the ending after feedback because the first version explained too much.#Incorrect. This explains revision.
=C. First the character walks, then talks, then thinks, then goes home, and that is the whole story.#Correct. This retells events without craft reasoning.
~D. As you read, notice the repeated hallway image.#Incorrect. This frames the audience.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q05::Which audience frame is strongest?{
=A. As you read, notice how the repeated hallway image changes from safety to pressure.#Correct. This gives the audience a specific craft feature to notice.
~B. Notice everything and nothing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. I will tell you the ending before you read.#Incorrect. This may replace the reading experience.
~D. Ignore the images.#Incorrect. This does not support craft understanding.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q06::A student says, "I revised because the teacher said to." What is missing?{
~A. A longer plot summary.#Incorrect. The issue is revision reasoning, not plot summary.
~B. A random new title.#Incorrect. A title does not solve missing revision reasoning.
~C. A list of every comma.#Incorrect. The problem is not just conventions.
=D. An explanation of what changed and why the final version is stronger.#Correct. Revision reflection must explain improvement.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q07::Which revision reflection is strongest?{
~A. I changed things randomly.#Incorrect. This does not show purposeful revision.
=B. I cut the first paragraph because it explained the conflict before the reader could experience it.#Correct. This names a change and explains the effect.
~C. I did not read the feedback.#Incorrect. That does not show revision reflection.
~D. I added more words because more is always better.#Incorrect. Length alone does not prove improvement.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q08::Which presentation order best matches the lesson?{
~A. Ending, unrelated memory, no title, no purpose.#Incorrect. This does not guide the audience.
~B. Full plot summary, every symbol, no revision.#Incorrect. This overexplains and misses revision reflection.
=C. Title and form, purpose, craft choices, revision decision, audience frame.#Correct. This is the lesson's presentation sequence.
~D. Font choice only.#Incorrect. Font choice alone is not an author presentation.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q09::Which statement best keeps the creative work central?{
~A. I will explain every possible meaning before anyone reads.#Incorrect. This replaces the creative work.
~B. There is no need to read the piece after my presentation.#Incorrect. The presentation should support reading.
~C. My summary is more important than the poem.#Incorrect. The creative work should remain central.
=D. I will point the audience to the repeated image, then let the poem create its own effect.#Correct. This guides the audience without replacing the work.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q10::Which choice is a useful craft choice to explain?{
=A. Point of view, because it shapes what the reader knows and feels.#Correct. Point of view is a meaningful craft choice.
~B. The fact that the page exists.#Incorrect. This is not a specific craft choice.
~C. The writer's lunch.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. A random file name.#Incorrect. This does not explain the creative work.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q11::A student says, "My poem is about sadness." How can this purpose statement be improved?{
~A. Make it longer by listing every line.#Incorrect. Length does not necessarily improve purpose.
~B. Remove the purpose entirely.#Incorrect. Purpose helps frame the presentation.
=C. Explain the specific effect, such as showing sadness through ordinary objects rather than direct statements.#Correct. This gives a clearer intended effect.
~D. Replace it with "I do not know."#Incorrect. That does not frame the audience.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q12::Which response best explains an author's title choice?{
~A. I picked the title randomly.#Incorrect. This does not show craft reasoning.
=B. I chose "Open Door" because the door image represents both invitation and uncertainty.#Correct. This explains the title's effect.
~C. Titles never matter.#Incorrect. Titles can prepare the audience.
~D. I used the title to avoid revising.#Incorrect. A title does not replace revision.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q13::Which is not enough for mastery in this lesson?{
=A. A presentation that only says "I like my piece."#Correct. This lacks purpose, craft reasoning, and revision reflection.
~B. A presentation that names purpose and form.#Incorrect. That supports mastery.
~C. A presentation that explains two craft choices.#Incorrect. That supports mastery.
~D. A presentation that reflects on revision.#Incorrect. That supports mastery.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q14::Which sentence best connects feedback to revision?{
~A. I ignored all feedback.#Incorrect. This does not connect feedback to improvement.
~B. Feedback exists.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. I changed the whole piece because someone spoke.#Incorrect. This does not show thoughtful revision.
=D. Peer feedback showed the ending was too direct, so I replaced the explanation with an action that suggests the feeling.#Correct. This explains feedback, revision, and effect.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q15::Which presentation language is clearest and most author-focused?{
~A. Stuff happened and I wrote it.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. It is whatever you think.#Incorrect. This avoids author reasoning.
=C. I used first-person narration so the reader would experience the speaker's uncertainty directly.#Correct. This explains a craft choice and reader effect.
~D. I cannot explain anything.#Incorrect. The lesson requires explanation.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q16::What should an author presentation avoid?{
~A. Naming the form.#Incorrect. Naming the form is useful.
=B. Overexplaining every symbol so the audience has no interpretive work left.#Correct. The presentation should guide, not replace, the creative work.
~C. Explaining a revision decision.#Incorrect. That is expected.
~D. Giving an audience frame.#Incorrect. That helps the audience enter the piece.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q17::Which reflection focuses on product and process?{
~A. I finished.#Incorrect. This does not explain process or product.
~B. Writing happened.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. My draft exists.#Incorrect. This lacks reflection.
=D. I changed the pacing after feedback, and the final version creates more tension before the reveal.#Correct. This connects process to final product.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q18::Which author statement best explains line breaks?{
=A. I broke the final phrase onto its own line so the reader would pause before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This explains format and reader effect.
~B. I used line breaks because poems have lines.#Incorrect. This does not explain a specific effect.
~C. I added lines randomly.#Incorrect. Random choices are not strong craft reasoning.
~D. I removed all structure.#Incorrect. This does not explain purposeful line breaks.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q19::What should the author do if the presentation becomes too long because it summarizes every event?{
=A. Focus on purpose, two craft choices, and one revision decision.#Correct. This brings the presentation back to the lesson sequence.
~B. Add more summary.#Incorrect. Summary is already the problem.
~C. Remove all craft reasoning.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
~D. Avoid mentioning the creative work at all.#Incorrect. The presentation should still frame the work.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q20::Which sentence is the best revision of "My story is good"?{
~A. My story is good because it is good.#Incorrect. This is circular and vague.
~B. My story is good, and that is all.#Incorrect. This lacks craft reasoning.
=C. The repeated door image helps show the character's hesitation, especially in the final paragraph.#Correct. This replaces vague judgment with craft evidence.
~D. My story has pages.#Incorrect. This does not explain quality.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q21::Which element belongs in the checkpoint task?{
=A. Two craft explanations with reader effects.#Correct. This is part of the author presentation plan.
~B. Private personal information.#Incorrect. That is not required and may be inappropriate.
~C. A full summary of every event only.#Incorrect. Summary alone is not enough.
~D. A peer's name as the main point.#Incorrect. The focus is author reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q22::Which statement best shows audience awareness?{
~A. I do not care what readers notice.#Incorrect. Audience awareness matters.
~B. I will hide the purpose from everyone.#Incorrect. The presentation should frame the audience.
=C. As you read, notice how the sound details become quieter as the speaker calms down.#Correct. This guides readers to a craft pattern.
~D. The audience should not read the piece.#Incorrect. The presentation supports reading.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q23::Which is the best reason to mention revision in an author presentation?{
~A. To prove the first draft was bad.#Incorrect. Revision is about improvement, not shame.
=B. To show how a purposeful change improved the final work.#Correct. This connects process and product.
~C. To avoid explaining craft.#Incorrect. Revision does not replace craft reasoning.
~D. To make the presentation longer for no reason.#Incorrect. Length is not the goal.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q24::Which presentation response should receive corrective feedback?{
~A. I used dialogue to show conflict without explaining it directly.#Incorrect. This is strong craft reasoning.
=B. I cannot name a purpose, craft choice, or revision decision.#Correct. This misses the required components.
~C. I revised the ending so the final image carries the emotion.#Incorrect. This is a useful revision explanation.
~D. As you read, notice how the hallway image changes.#Incorrect. This is a useful audience frame.
}

::CW_U05_L06_Q25::Which mastery response best fits Lesson 6?{
=A. My presentation names the title and form, explains the purpose, describes two craft choices, and reflects on one revision decision.#Correct. This includes the core mastery components.
~B. My presentation only retells the plot.#Incorrect. Plot summary alone is not enough.
~C. My presentation says nothing about craft.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
~D. My presentation avoids revision because revision is private.#Incorrect. Revision reflection is part of the lesson.
}
